#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef2ab87-f78b-5cbd-9be4-0c1ac3448d89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61ee586d-0612-5c3e-a75f-270583bfa339" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(9/2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61ee586d-0612-5c3e-a75f-270583bfa339" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(9/2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327821} :answer-field/id #uuid "74826e0f-e86b-5ce8-b382-97a7cd0aa465" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e117787-42d5-505b-9374-c2505266a725" :question/math-academy-id "q-128340" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2x^2-5x-3}$. Evaluate the improper integral:\n\n$$\\int_4^\\infty f(x)\\,dx=$$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For $R>4$, the partial-fraction identity gives\n\n$$\\int_4^R\\frac{7}{2x^2-5x-3}\\,dx=\\int_4^R\\frac{dx}{x-3}-2\\int_4^R\\frac{dx}{2x+1}.$$\n\nBoth terms have the same finite upper bound. Integrating and combining the logarithms before taking a limit yields\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_4^R\\frac{7}{2x^2-5x-3}\\,dx\n&=\\left[\\ln(x-3)-\\ln(2x+1)\\right]_4^R\\\\\n&=\\ln\\frac{R-3}{2R+1}+\\ln9\n=\\ln\\frac{9(R-3)}{2R+1}.\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\\int_4^\\infty\\frac{7}{2x^2-5x-3}\\,dx\n=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\ln\\frac{9(R-3)}{2R+1}\n=\\ln\\frac92.$$\n\nThe limit is finite, so the improper integral converges. The two partial-fraction improper integrals each diverge when considered separately; keeping a common finite upper bound avoids an undefined subtraction."}]}
